(Run a program, and learn how it died.

 A fuzzed program can meet three fates and the harness must tell them
 apart exactly: it exits with a code of its own, a signal kills it, or it
 never finishes and the harness has to end it. All three are read from the
 kernel's own wait status, so no fate can be mistaken for another.

 The decoders are written out here rather than taken from vox-libs'
 process library on purpose: the fuzzer must run on a machine with nothing
 installed, and these are four lines of arithmetic.)

To 'exit code of' with a number called status.
  Return a number, status divide 256 modulo 256.

To 'signal of' with a number called status.
  Return a number, status bit-and 127.

To crashed with a number called status.
  a number called 'terminating signal' is status bit-and 127.
  Return a boolean, 'terminating signal' is not 0.

Set 'the last status' to -1.

(A killed child may itself be a shell with a still-running grandchild -
 supervise's own SIGKILL only ever reaches the direct child. /proc's
 per-task children list is a plain file, so this needs no shell and no
 process-group plumbing Vox does not expose: read it, and if a grandchild
 is named, kill that too. Absence (no such path, no listed child) is the
 common case and is silently fine - there is nothing orphaned to chase.)
(Program level and reused across calls (Defect 17): a fresh declaration
 per call is never freed until program exit, and `Read` already resets
 a buffer's contents rather than appending to them, so nothing here
 needs an explicit `clear`.)

a buffer called 'the killed grandchild data'.

To 'kill grandchild of' with a number called pid.
    a text called 'the children path' is "/proc/{pid}/task/{pid}/children".
    open a file for reading called 'the children file' at 'the children path'.
    On error Return a number, 0.
    Read from 'the children file' into 'the killed grandchild data'.
    Close 'the children file'.
    a number called 'the grandchild pid' is 'the killed grandchild data' as a number.
    If 'the grandchild pid' is greater than 0 then,
        Send signal 9 to process 'the grandchild pid'.
    Return a number, 'the grandchild pid'.

(The reap loop, shared by supervise and 'supervise capturing': poll the
 forked child without blocking, kill it (and any grandchild) past the
 deadline, and read its true wait status. Split out so the two callers
 cannot drift apart on the one piece of logic that tells an exit code of
 139 from a real signal 11 - see the module comment at the top of this
 file.)
To 'reap with deadline' with a number called 'the child' and a number called 'the deadline in milliseconds'.
  a timer called clock.
  Start the clock.
  a number called 'reap result' is 0.
  a boolean called 'the child is still running' is true.
  a boolean called 'the child was killed' is false.
  While 'the child is still running',
      Set 'reap result' to reap child 'the child' without waiting,
      If 'reap result' is 'the child' then,
          Set 'the child is still running' to false.
      If 'the child is still running' then,
          a number called 'milliseconds waited' is the clock's elapsed in milliseconds,
          If 'milliseconds waited' is greater than 'the deadline in milliseconds' then,
              a number called 'the killed grandchild' is 'kill grandchild of' of 'the child',
              Send signal 9 to process 'the child',
              Set 'reap result' to reap child 'the child',
              Set 'the child is still running' to false,
              Set 'the child was killed' to true.
      Wait 5 milliseconds.
  Set 'the last status' to the reaped status.
  If 'the child was killed' then,
      Return a text, "hang".
  If crashed of 'the last status' then,
      Return a text, "signal".
  Return a text, "exit".

(Run a program to completion, or to the deadline, whichever comes first.
 Returns how it ended; the raw wait status is left in 'the last status'.

 The argument list is argv[1..]: Vox prepends the program path as argv[0]
 itself, so naming it again in the list passes it to the program as a real
 argument.)
To supervise with a text called program and a list called 'the argument list' and a number called 'the deadline in milliseconds'.
  a number called 'the child' is fork the process.
  If 'the child' is 0 then,
      Execute program with arguments 'the argument list',
      Exit 127.
  a text called 'the verdict' is 'reap with deadline' of 'the child' and 'the deadline in milliseconds'.
  Return a text, 'the verdict'.

(Same as supervise, but the child's stdout is redirected to 'the capture
 path' before Execute - the differential oracle's prerequisite (defect 9)
 and defect 7's stdin fix both need this same plumbing, so it is built
 once as a general capability rather than wired only for the oracle.

 No dup2 in the language (LANGUAGE.md's process/FD surface stops at
 fork/reap/Execute and numeric-fd borrowing), so this uses the classic
 POSIX substitute: borrow the real fd 1, Close it (a real close(2) - the
 tracking table exists for cleanup, not to fake the syscall away, per
 FILE_CLOSE in coreasm/x86_64/file.asm), then open the capture file. The
 kernel always hands out the LOWEST free descriptor, and 1 is now it, so
 the new file becomes the child's stdout with no dup2 needed. This runs
 strictly after fork, in the child's own copy of the fd table - the
 parent's real stdout (fd 1) is untouched, so nothing here can repeat the
 shared-offset corruption defect 7 documents from the stdin side (a child
 writing through a fd it shares with the parent moves the parent's own
 file position). Each call opens a FRESH file (O_TRUNC, no append), so
 two calls - even back to back for the same program, which is exactly
 what the oracle does - never share an open file description with each
 other either.)
(Stdin, redirected from a file before Execute — defect 7's fix, and
 plan 327's prerequisite.

 Without it the child inherits the FUZZER's stdin, so a generated
 program reading /dev/stdin blocks until the deadline and is classified
 `hang`: a false finding on every program carrying one. Proved before
 building this: with `< /dev/null` such a read returns 0 bytes and exits
 0; with an open-but-silent pipe it blocks until killed, and the second
 is what an inherited terminal or pipe gives you.

 The mechanism is the same one the stdout capture already uses — close
 the descriptor, then open, and the open takes the lowest free number,
 which is the one just freed. Verified in isolation with /bin/cat before
 being wired in here.

 An empty input path means /dev/null: a program that reads gets a clean
 immediate EOF rather than the fuzzer's own stdin. That is the SAFE
 default and is what every existing caller gets.)
To 'supervise capturing with input' with a text called program and a list called 'the argument list' and a number called 'the deadline in milliseconds' and a text called 'the capture path' and a text called 'the input path'.
  a text called 'the stdin source' is "/dev/null".
  If 'the input path' is not empty then,
      Set 'the stdin source' to 'the input path'.
  a number called 'the child' is fork the process.
  If 'the child' is 0 then,
      open a file for reading called 'stdin borrow' at 0,
      Close 'stdin borrow',
      open a file for reading called 'the injected input' at 'the stdin source',
      open a file for writing called 'stdout borrow' at 1,
      Close 'stdout borrow',
      open a file for writing called 'the captured output' at 'the capture path',
      Execute program with arguments 'the argument list',
      Exit 127.
  a text called 'the verdict' is 'reap with deadline' of 'the child' and 'the deadline in milliseconds'.
  Return a text, 'the verdict'.

To 'supervise capturing' with a text called program and a list called 'the argument list' and a number called 'the deadline in milliseconds' and a text called 'the capture path'.
  a text called 'no input' is "".
  a text called 'the verdict' is 'supervise capturing with input' of program and 'the argument list' and 'the deadline in milliseconds' and 'the capture path' and 'no input'.
  Return a text, 'the verdict'.

(Read a whole file back into text, for comparing two captured runs. Sets
 'the last read succeeded' so a caller can tell a genuinely empty file
 (legitimate - a program that never Prints) from a read that failed
 (plumbing broke) - collapsing both to "" would let a read failure on
 one side and real content on the other masquerade as a nondeterminism
 finding, which is exactly the false positive this oracle must not
 produce.)
a boolean called 'the last read succeeded' is false.

(Program level and reused across calls (Defect 17): `Read` resets a
 buffer's contents rather than appending, so no explicit `clear` is
 needed between calls.)

a buffer called 'the whole file read bytes'.

To 'read whole file' with a text called path.
    Set 'the last read succeeded' to false.
    open a file for reading called 'the file to read' at path.
    On error Return a text, "".
    Read from 'the file to read' into 'the whole file read bytes'.
    On error Return a text, "".
    Close 'the file to read'.
    Set 'the last read succeeded' to true.
    a text called 'the read text' is 'the whole file read bytes' as text.
    Return a text, 'the read text'.

(Run a shell command, for the two things Vox cannot do itself: redirecting
 a child's output to a file, and line surgery with sed. Never use this to
 run a fuzzed program - supervise does that, and only supervise can tell an
 exit code of 139 from a death by signal 11.)
To 'run shell' with a text called command and a number called 'the deadline in milliseconds'.
  a list called 'the shell arguments' is [].
  append "-c" to 'the shell arguments'.
  append command to 'the shell arguments'.
  a text called 'how it ended' is supervise of "/bin/sh" and 'the shell arguments' and 'the deadline in milliseconds'.
  If 'how it ended' is not "exit" then,
      Return a number, 0 minus 1.
  Return a number, 'exit code of' of 'the last status'.

(The one place a value that did not originate in this source gets spliced
 into a 'run shell' string. Single-quotes the whole value and closes/reopens
 the quote around any embedded quote (' becomes '\''), which is the standard
 POSIX-shell escape - so the result is always exactly one shell word, no
 matter what bytes it holds. --out/--vox/--core reach 'run shell' only
 through this function; that makes it the one place to audit, per plan
 322 A2.)
To 'shell quote' with a text called value.
    a buffer called 'the source bytes' is value.
    a buffer called 'the quoted bytes' is "'".
    a number called 'the scan position' is 1.
    a number called 'the byte count' is 'the source bytes's size.
    a buffer called 'one byte' is " ".
    While 'the scan position' is less than or equal to 'the byte count',
        a number called 'the current byte' is byte 'the scan position' of 'the source bytes',
        If 'the current byte' is 39 then,
            append "'\\''" to 'the quoted bytes',
        Otherwise,
            Set byte 1 of 'one byte' to 'the current byte',
            append 'one byte' to 'the quoted bytes'.
        Set 'the scan position' to 'the scan position' add 1.
    append "'" to 'the quoted bytes'.
    a text called result is 'the quoted bytes' as text.
    Return a text, result.

(mkdir -p, without a shell: 'Create a directory called' is one mkdir(2)
 call, so a path whose parent does not exist yet fails. Walk the path
 byte by byte and create each ancestor as its closing '/' is seen, then
 the whole path last. This is what lets findings.vox create a
 user-controlled --out directory (however it is spelled - nested, spaced,
 or holding characters that would have been shell syntax) without ever
 handing it to /bin/sh.)
To 'make directories' with a text called path.
    a buffer called 'the path bytes' is path.
    a number called 'the byte count' is 'the path bytes's size.
    a number called 'the scan position' is 1.
    a buffer called prefix.
    a buffer called 'one path byte' is " ".
    While 'the scan position' is less than or equal to 'the byte count',
        a number called 'the current byte' is byte 'the scan position' of 'the path bytes',
        a number called 'prefix so far length' is prefix's size,
        If 'the current byte' is 47 and 'prefix so far length' is greater than 0 then,
            a text called ancestor is prefix as text,
            Create a directory called ancestor.
        Set byte 1 of 'one path byte' to 'the current byte',
        append 'one path byte' to prefix,
        Set 'the scan position' to 'the scan position' add 1.
    a text called 'the whole path' is prefix as text.
    Create a directory called 'the whole path'.
    Return a number, 0.